          "short": "Recognize fruit disorders and leaf spots early",
          "guidance": "On outdoor garden tomatoes, blossom-end rot appears as a flattened or corky tan-to-black patch at the blossom end; soil-moisture swings can contribute, but the symptom alone does not prove calcium-deficient soil. Cracks may radiate from the stem or encircle fruit after rapid growth with heavy rain or heat, or after watering resumes following drought. Keep moisture more even with regular root-zone watering and mulch. Brown or black leaf spots often begin on lower leaves; early blight, Septoria, and bacterial spot can look alike early. Keep foliage dry, support plants for airflow, remove affected leaves promptly, and confirm the diagnosis before targeted treatment. UMN reports that large slicer types are generally more prone to blossom-end rot than cherry types; this is a crop-type comparison, not a prediction about Black Cherry. This is outdoor garden guidance, not a hydroponic diagnosis.",
