          "guidance": "USU describes blackheart as gradual black discoloration and death of young leaves at the plant center, associated with a localized calcium disorder; it recommends avoiding water stress as risk reduction. It also describes powdery mildew as white patches beginning on older leaves, and aphids as soft-bodied insects under leaves associated with curling or crinkling. These signs warrant inspection, not an automatic diagnosis from appearance alone. The advice is crop-level soil-garden evidence, not cultivar-specific resistance information.",
