          "guidance": "WSU recommends full sun and a well-drained garden site; waterlogging can stunt plants and contribute to damping-off. Soil moisture is especially important during flowering and grain fill, while high heat at flowering can impair seed formation. In WSU's regional evidence, temperatures above 95°F during flowering caused severe yield loss in eastern Washington, with losses also reported in Colorado and Minnesota; this is not a universal cutoff. Avoid excess nitrogen, which WSU links to delayed maturity, excess foliage and lodging. For containers, WSU cautions that the smaller soil volume offers less buffering against fertilizer toxicity and recommends checking the potting mix's initial nutrient level before adding more. These are soil/container crop recommendations, not hydroponic compatibility.",
